The 1949 Île de France breakfast menu listed these fruits: honeydews, grapefruit, red raisins (grapes), white raisins (grapes), oranges, pears, bananas, apples. 

A high-resolution image of Janet Fish’s 2008 painting “Embroidery from Uzbekistan” clearly shows (moving clockwise, starting from the top center):
12 o’clock – a cluster of green grapes spilling from a glass bowl;
about 2 o’clock – two bright oranges beside the bowl’s rim;
about 4 o’clock – a yellow-green pear resting partly on the embroidered cloth;
about 7 o’clock – a pair of red apples near the lower edge;
about 10 o’clock – more grapes curving back up to the top.

Among those, the fruits that also appear on the 1949 menu are grapes, oranges, pears, and apples.  Arranged clockwise beginning at 12 o’clock they are: grapes (top), oranges (upper right), pears (lower right), apples (lower left).
